- policy/AI_IO_ROUND_TRIP_AUTOPUBLISH_V1.json
- state/authorization/delegations/OWNER_AI_IO_ROUND_TRIP_AUTOPUBLISH_V1.json

Every item crossing a conforming QIK-VRT input/output boundary, regardless of modality, MUST receive a repository-bound persistence receipt. This includes human input, artificial-cognitive output, tool input/output, attachments, audio, images, documents, retrieved sources, decisions, reviews and external-effect results.

The interface invariant is:

INPUT/OUTPUT → EVENT RECEIPT → WORK UNIT → CLAIM GRANULARIZATION → NOVELTY/CONNECTIVITY → APPROPRIATE MACHINE PROOF → ZENODO CANDIDATE → EXACT BYTE FREEZE → SINGLE-USE EFFECT AUTHORIZATION → ZENODO → POST-EFFECT RECEIPT → IETF ONLY FOR A NORMATIVE PROTOCOL OR INTEROPERABILITY DELTA → NEXT DIFFERENCE.

No secret, credential or private key is persisted as raw repository content. Where rights, privacy or confidentiality prohibit raw-byte persistence, retain the exact digest, provenance, classification and reason instead; silent dropping is forbidden.

Every conforming AI client MUST persist its own substantive output before treating that output as a durable QIK-VRT result. A client unable to persist must report the I/O result as `UNPERSISTED_TRANSPORT_OUTPUT`, not repository truth. Event aggregation must preserve membership of all underlying event receipts.

New knowledge is not inferred from novelty of wording. A publishable claim requires machine-resolvable assumptions, scope, dependencies, source events, evidence, relation to existing claims and novelty basis. It must be proved using the proof class appropriate to its claim kind. Formal derivability, reproducible computation and empirical correspondence remain distinct proof/evidence classes.

The standing Product Owner delegation permits the automation, without repeated human questions, to derive a candidate-specific single-use exact-artifact authorization only after final bytes, metadata, machine proof and prepublication return receipt exist. Secure runtime credentials remain outside the repository. The existing generic Zenodo publisher is reused. IETF routing is required only when a normative protocol, wire, state-machine or interoperability specification changes.

